<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 188)</h2>
<p><b>Client / Reviewer mode.</b> Invite someone into a review by name, at the access level you choose, and send them a single file to open.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Session ▸ Invite a Reviewer…</b> — name them, pick their tier, save the invite. Opening it puts them in the review: session, passcode, host address and identity all carried, nothing to configure. Their name comes from the invite, so their first comment is attributed correctly.</li>
<li><b>Reviewer</b> — playback, compare, annotation, comments, marking a preferred version, note and report export, and the looks you saved to the session bank.</li>
<li><b>Expert reviewer</b> — all of that plus the review grade, printer lights, scopes, their own LUTs and Film Lab. For directors who like to tinker.</li>
<li>Neither tier reaches the pipeline: no conform, no relink, no packaging.</li>
<li>A guest sees their tier at the bottom-left of the viewport, and <b>Session ▸ Sign Out of Review</b> returns their copy to full mode.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Where a shared review reaches:</b> the same LAN, a <b>Tailscale</b> network, or a VPN — anywhere the guest's Mac can open a connection to yours. Invites now carry the host's address and prefer a Tailscale one, because browsing by name uses Bonjour and Bonjour is multicast, which a tailnet doesn't carry. There's no internet relay yet, so a client with no VPN and no tailnet still can't join a live session — send them a session package for those.</p>
<p>Also: a connection that can't reach the host now says so instead of sitting on "Connecting…", and the dead plain-1–4 zoom keys are gone (those digits pick the compare set; zoom is ⌘1–⌘4).</p>
]]></description>
